 ## Overview
-Compositing and Blending defines the mathematical and algorithmic rules for combining sample contributions $(c_i, \alpha_i)$ along a ray into a final pixel color. It solves the problem of optical integration, determining how emitted/scattered light and absorption interact to form the visible image. It sets itself apart from its siblings by focusing on the specific accumulation logic (e.g., standard alpha blending, intensity projection, or parallel distribution strategies) rather than the ray traversal or post-processing stages.
+Compositing and Blending defines the final stage of direct volume ray casting where per-ray sample contributions (color and opacity) are integrated into a 2D pixel value [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of correctly blending semi-transparent, overlapping density fields to produce physically plausible or semantically meaningful visualizations, handling tasks ranging from standard optical attenuation to differentiable training gradients and object-level importance modulation [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is its focus on the mathematical integration of per-ray sample attributes (color, opacity, density) into a final pixel value, often leveraging specific mathematical forms (polynomials, kernels) or optimization strategies (early termination, packed layouts) rather than just acceleration structures or post-processing filters.
 
