+Layered SDF Compositing is a technique for rendering Signed Distance Field (SDF) glyphs by compositing multiple pre-multiplied alpha layers (typically a face and one or more outline layers) into a final pixel color. It solves the problem of rendering crisp, scalable text or icons with anti-aliased edges and colored outlines by treating each visual component as a distinct alpha-blended layer. It differs from standard SDF rendering by explicitly managing a fixed number of discrete layers (1, 2, or 4) using specific linear interpolation logic on pre-multiplied colors, rather than a continuous ray-casting integration. See [Layered SDF Compositing](3641-layered-sdf-compositing.md).
 
 ### [Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques](4646-reconstruction-and-compositing-techniques.md)
